<BaseLayout title="About"><section class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6 pt-16 pb-24"><p class="kicker">About / Boyuan (Ben) Wang</p><h1 class="text-4xl mt-6 mb-8">A bit about me.</h1><div class="prose"><p>I’m a mechanical engineering M.S. student at Duke, graduating in December 2026, and a graduate researcher in the General Robotics Lab. I earned my B.S. in Mechanical Engineering at Ohio State, with a minor in Computer and Information Science.</p><p>I like the part where a design becomes something you can put on a bench and try. A mechanism that moves well in CAD still has to be assembled, wired, controlled, and tested. That’s where much of my work happens.</p><p>I’m also interested in what a machine can learn from what it sees or hears. On the robotic hand project, I worked on the hardware, simulation, and control setup and helped get the visual inference pipeline running. For our intubation capstone, I built the testbed for studying acoustic signals from tube–tissue contact; my teammates worked on denoising and learning-based analysis.</p><p>My ultimate goal is to help build care robots that can look after me when I’m retired. There’s a personal deadline in there somewhere.</p><p>Outside of engineering, I like hiking. It’s a good reason to step away from the workbench for a while.</p><h2>What I work with</h2><p>Most designs start in SolidWorks or Fusion, then move to the shop for machining, 3D printing, and assembly. I use GD&amp;T to communicate how parts need to fit, and do the wiring and soldering needed to bring a prototype to life.</p><p>For robot models and simulation, I work with URDF, PyBullet, and MuJoCo. Python is useful for trying ideas, running experiments, and working with data; I also use C++ and ROS / ROS 2 for control and integration.</p><h2>Contact</h2><p><a href="mailto:bw277@duke.edu">bw277@duke.edu</a> · <a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/boyuan-ben-wang/">LinkedIn</a></p></div></section></BaseLayout>
